            {
              "id": "glog-f24e8cb6-f814-4f68-91d2-22ff46dd8fd3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is used to copy a string from source to destination. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function does not guarantee to null-terminate the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the destination string size. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, cause a denial of service, or gain unauthorized access.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string copy functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. Functions such as `strlcpy` or `snprintf` can be used instead. Also, always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string including the null-terminating character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `strlcpy` or `snprintf` function. Here is an example of how to do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nchar hostHash[SIZE];\nchar hostname[SIZE];\n\n// strncpy(hostHash, hostname, sizeof(hostHash));\nsnprintf(hostHash, sizeof(hostHash), \"%s\", hostname);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `string.h`\n- `stdio.h`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2221f9d4-457f-4946-9d9d-fc071f28f5de",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or if the return value of `malloc` is not checked, it can lead to buffer overflows, null pointer dereferences, or other memory corruption issues. These can potentially be exploited to execute arbitrary code, crash the program, or leak sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc`. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer. Using this pointer without checking can lead to null pointer dereferences.\n\n2. Validate the size of the memory to be allocated. If the size is not properly validated, it can lead to integer overflows or underflows, which can in turn lead to buffer overflows.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using memory-safe languages or libraries that automatically manage memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the code:\n\n```c++\nsendRegHandles = (RegHandle*) malloc(sizeof(*sendRegHandles));\nif (sendRegHandles == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks if `malloc` returns a null pointer and handles the error if it does.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-29cf2444-4974-4a25-ab97-16a47b25f5bb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. `malloc` is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The problem arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or can be influenced by an attacker. This can lead to several types of vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, integer overflows, or even denial of service attacks.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `malloc(totalProcs * MAX_LINE)`, if the `totalProcs` variable is controlled by the user or can be influenced by an attacker, it can lead to an integer overflow vulnerability. If `totalProcs` is sufficiently large, the multiplication can result in a value that is too large to be represented in the data type of the result. This can cause the actual amount of memory allocated to be less than expected, leading to buffer overflows.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Validate and sanitize all user inputs. Never trust data provided by the user.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the data written to buffers.\n3. Use modern memory management techniques and libraries that can help prevent these types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for this vulnerability would be to check for integer overflow before calling `malloc`. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```cpp\nif (totalProcs > INT_MAX / MAX_LINE) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(totalProcs * MAX_LINE);\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we check if the multiplication would result in an integer overflow. If it would, we handle the error instead of calling `malloc`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
